  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. Reading both back from Kibana confirmed they exist with the expected settings. The rule's status is still \"pending\" because it hasn't run for the first time yet.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` (time field `timestamp`) holds web server access logs. Each document is one request and includes:\n- `response`: the HTTP status code, stored as a string, so I filtered on `response.keyword`\n- `bytes`: the response size\n- `clientip`: the client's IP address\n- `url`, `request`, `extension`: what was requested\n- `agent`, `machine.os`: the client's browser and operating system\n- `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`: location\n- `referer`, `tags`, `host`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-37db6e91 Web logs overview\"** (7 panels, default time range last 7 days): http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-37db6e91-web-logs-overview-526f52845f4e\n- **Single-number panels:** total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs), and total bytes served.\n- **Requests over time:** a bar chart over time, split by response code.\n- **Response code breakdown:** a pie chart.\n- **Top requested URLs:** a table with request count and average bytes per URL.\n- **Traffic by source country:** a bar chart.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-37db6e91 5xx spike\"** (id `177e7b1b-2748-49b0-a929-cce48b6a54c0`) is an Elasticsearch query rule:\n- It searches `kibana_sample_data_logs` for documents whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\", meaning any 5xx code.\n- It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 such requests have arrived in the last 5 minutes.\n- It has no actions attached, so a firing alert only shows up in Kibana and nothing is sent anywhere. If you want notifications, a connector such as Slack, email or server log would need to be added.\n\n`KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` was empty in the shell, so I couldn't call the API directly. I did all of this through the Kibana tools instead.",
